datasets.description:
Networks of products sold on Amazon.com and their co-purchasing relations, collected by J. Leskovec, L. Adamic and B. Huberman by crawling the Amazon website in 2003. The data are based on the "Customers Who Bought This Item Also Bought" feature of the product pages: a directed edge $i \to j$ means that product $j$ is listed on the page of product $i$ as frequently co-purchased with it. Nodes are products (e.g. books, music CDs, DVDs and VHS video tapes).

There are four snapshots, crawled on different dates:

- `302`: March 2, 2003 (at most 5 out-edges per product);
- `312`: March 12, 2003;
- `505`: May 5, 2003;
- `601`: June 1, 2003.

In the last three snapshots each product has at most 10 out-edges. Node identifiers are not consistent across snapshots, and the networks have no node or edge properties.

Co-authorship networks of scientists who submitted papers to five categories of the arXiv e-print repository, compiled by J. Leskovec, J. Kleinberg and C. Faloutsos. The data cover papers submitted from January 1993 to April 2003 (124 months), beginning a few months after the inception of arXiv. Nodes are authors, and an undirected edge connects two authors if they co-authored at least one paper; a paper with $k$ authors thus generates a fully connected subgraph on $k$ nodes. Authors are identified by anonymous numeric identifiers, stored in the node property `name`, and the networks have no other node or edge properties. The few self-loops of the source edge lists are removed.

There is one network per arXiv category:

- `AstroPh`: astrophysics (astro-ph);
- `CondMat`: condensed matter physics (cond-mat);
- `GrQc`: general relativity and quantum cosmology (gr-qc);
- `HepPh`: high energy physics, phenomenology (hep-ph);
- `HepTh`: high energy physics, theory (hep-th).

Networks of protein–protein interactions and other functional relationships between genes of the nematode *Caenorhabditis elegans*, from the study of N. Simonis and collaborators at the Center for Cancer Systems Biology (Dana-Farber Cancer Institute), which produced an empirically controlled, high-quality map of the *C. elegans* interactome. Nodes are genes (proteins), identified by their *C. elegans* sequence names (e.g. `AC7.2`), and an undirected edge connects two genes that are related in the corresponding source (a physical interaction between their proteins, a genetic interaction, correlated expression or similar phenotypes, see below). There are ten networks:

- `wi2004`: protein interactions from the first high-throughput yeast two-hybrid (Y2H) map of Li et al. (2004);
- `wi2007`: protein interactions from the new Y2H screens of Simonis et al. (Worm Interactome version 2007);
- `BPmaps`: Y2H interactions from earlier smaller-scale "biological process maps" focused on specific processes;
- `WI8`: the union of `wi2004`, `wi2007` and `BPmaps`;
- `LCI`: literature-curated interactions supported by small-scale experiments;
- `Interolog`: interactions predicted from interactions between orthologous proteins in other species;
- `Genetic`: genetic interactions;
- `Microarray`: pairs of genes with highly correlated expression profiles in microarray experiments;
- `Phenotypes`: pairs of genes with similar phenotypic profiles (e.g. in RNAi experiments);
- `IntegratedNetwork`: the union of all of the above sources.

The networks have the following properties:

- (Node) `name`: the gene's sequence name.
- (Edge) `pcc` (`Microarray`): the Pearson correlation coefficient of the expression profiles of the two genes.
- (Edge) `phenotypes` (`Phenotypes`): the phenotypic similarity score of the two genes.
- (Edge) `wi2004`, `wi2007` and `bpmaps` (`WI8`): the status of the interaction in each of the three source data sets (e.g. `core` or `noncore`; empty if the interaction is not in the data set).
- (Edge) `wi8`, `lci`, `interologs` and `genetic` (`IntegratedNetwork`): whether the edge is present in the corresponding source network.
- (Edge) `phenotypes` and `coexpression` (`IntegratedNetwork`): the phenotypic similarity score and the expression correlation of the two genes (NaN if not available).

The networks come from the *C. elegans* download page of the CCSB Interactome Database, whose other data sets are in the entries `yeast_interactome_ccsb` and `collins_yeast` (yeast) and `interactome_vidal` and `human_interactome_2014` (human).

Ego networks of users of Facebook, Google+ and Twitter, collected by J. McAuley and J. Leskovec to study how users organize their friends into social circles. An ego network consists of the friends of a user (the ego) and the connections among them, where the ego is not included as a node. The Facebook data were collected from survey participants through a Facebook app, and include the users' manually labeled circles (friend lists). The Google+ data were crawled in 2012 from users who had manually shared their circles using the "share circle" feature, and the Twitter data from public sources (lists). Node features (profile information) are given as binary vectors; for Facebook, the feature values have been anonymized, so that only the kind of feature (e.g. `birthday`, `education;school;id`) can be identified.

There are several networks:

- `facebook_<ego>`: the 10 Facebook ego networks (undirected), named by the identifier of the ego;
- `facebook_combined`: the union of all Facebook ego networks (undirected, including the egos), with 4,039 users;
- `gplus_<ego>`: the Google+ ego networks (directed), named by the identifier of the ego (the 131 ego networks of the source with at least one edge);
- `gplus_combined`: the union of all Google+ ego networks (directed), with 107,614 users;
- `twitter_<ego>`: the 973 Twitter ego networks (directed), named by the identifier of the ego;
- `twitter_combined`: the union of all Twitter ego networks (directed), with 81,306 users.

Nodes are users. In Facebook, an undirected edge means that the two users are friends; in Google+ and Twitter, a directed edge $i \to j$ means that user $i$ follows user $j$. For Twitter, the features are the hashtags (e.g. `#python`) and user mentions (e.g. `@nytimes`) used by the users. The features of the egos themselves are not included.

The networks have the following properties:

- (Node) `name` (ego networks): the identifier of the user.
- (Node) `feat` (ego networks): the binary feature vector of the user.
- (Node) `circles` (ego networks): the list of circles of the ego to which the user belongs, each identified by its index (starting from 0) in the circles file of the source data.
- (Graph) `feat_names` (ego networks): a dictionary mapping each position of the feature vector to the name of the feature (e.g. `gender:1` or `birthday;anonymized feature 0`).

Temporal networks of interactions among users of four question-and-answer websites of the Stack Exchange network: Stack Overflow (programming), Math Overflow (research mathematics), Super User (computer use) and Ask Ubuntu (the Ubuntu operating system). The networks were constructed by A. Paranjape, A. R. Benson and J. Leskovec from the public Stack Exchange data dump, and span from the creation of each site (2008 or 2009) until March 2016. Nodes are users, and a directed edge $(i,j)$ with timestamp $t$ indicates that user $i$ responded to a post of user $j$ at time $t$, in one of three ways:

- `a2q`: user $i$ answered user $j$'s question;
- `c2q`: user $i$ commented on user $j$'s question;
- `c2a`: user $i$ commented on user $j$'s answer;

and `all` is the union of the three types. Each network is named after the site and the type of interaction, as in `mathoverflow_a2q`. Each interaction is a separate edge, so pairs of users can be connected by many parallel edges, and the networks contain self-loops, e.g. when users comment on their own posts.

The networks named with `2024`, as in `mathoverflow_2024_a2q`, were constructed in the same way from the Stack Exchange data dump of April 2024 (the last one published on the Internet Archive), and span from the creation of each site until early April 2024. They contain the questions, answers and comments of the dump whose authors (and the authors of the posts they respond to) are given; the posts and comments of deleted user accounts, which the dump gives without author, are left out, as are those of the automated "Community" account (user id -1), which the 2016 networks do not contain either. In the `all` networks, the interactions are sorted by time.

The networks have the following properties:

- (Node) `vid`: the user ID from the source data.
- (Edge) `timestamp`: the time of the interaction (Unix time, in seconds).

Passing networks of soccer (association football) players, built from the match event data that StatsBomb (now part of Hudl) publishes as open data: all the 3,961 matches listed in the data, of 24 men's and women's competitions, one network per competition, with all its seasons:

- `african_cup_of_nations`: Africa Cup of Nations 2023 (52 matches);
- `bundesliga`: German Bundesliga, the 34 matches of Bayer Leverkusen in each of the seasons 2015/2016 and 2023/2024;
- `champions_league`: the UEFA Champions League (European Cup) finals of 17 seasons between 1970/1971 and 2018/2019, and one match of 1999/2000;
- `copa_america`: Copa América 2024 (32 matches);
- `copa_del_rey`: three finals of the Spanish Copa del Rey (1977/1978, 1982/1983 and 1983/1984);
- `fa_womens_super_league`: English FA Women's Super League, seasons 2018/2019 to 2020/2021 and 2023/2024 (457 matches);
- `fifa_u20_world_cup`: the final of the FIFA U20 World Cup 1979;
- `fifa_world_cup`: FIFA World Cups 2018 and 2022 (all 64 matches of each), and 19 matches of the World Cups 1958 to 1990;
- `frauen_bundesliga`: German Frauen-Bundesliga, season 2023/2024 (132 matches);
- `indian_super_league`: Indian Super League, season 2021/2022 (115 matches);
- `la_liga`: Spanish La Liga, all 380 matches of season 2015/2016, and the matches of Lionel Messi with FC Barcelona of 16 seasons between 2004/2005 and 2020/2021, plus one match of 1973/1974 (867 matches);
- `liga_f`: Spanish Liga F, season 2023/2024 (240 matches);
- `liga_profesional`: two matches of the Argentine Liga Profesional (1981 and 1997/1998);
- `ligue_1`: French Ligue 1, season 2015/2016 (377 matches), and matches of Paris Saint-Germain in 2021/2022 and 2022/2023 (435 matches);
- `major_league_soccer`: six matches of Inter Miami in Major League Soccer 2023;
- `north_american_league`: the final of the North American Soccer League 1977;
- `nwsl`: National Women's Soccer League (United States), 36 matches of season 2018 and 137 of season 2023;
- `premier_league`: English Premier League, season 2015/2016 (380 matches), and the 38 matches of Arsenal in 2003/2004;
- `serie_a`: Italian Serie A, season 2015/2016 (380 matches), and one match of 1986/1987;
- `serie_a_women`: Italian Serie A Women, season 2023/2024 (130 matches);
- `uefa_euro`: UEFA European Championships 2020 and 2024 (102 matches);
- `uefa_europa_league`: three matches of Napoli in the UEFA Cup 1988/1989;
- `uefa_womens_euro`: UEFA Women's European Championships 2022 and 2025 (62 matches);
- `womens_world_cup`: FIFA Women's World Cups 2019 and 2023 (116 matches).

Each node is a player, and each directed edge goes from a player to a teammate who received at least one of their completed passes in a given match, weighted by the number of such passes; the network of a competition is thus a multigraph, with one edge per pair of players and match, and the passing network of a single team in a single match is the subgraph of the edges with the corresponding `match_id` and `team`. Passes include set pieces (e.g. corners, free kicks, goal kicks and throw-ins). StatsBomb records the recipient of each pass; a pass is completed if it has no outcome (otherwise incomplete, out of play, offside, an injury clearance, or unknown). Of the 3,836,550 passes in the matches, the 2,973,962 completed ones are included; the others are left out. Only players who made or received at least one completed pass appear in the networks. The data also contain event files of 274 matches that are not listed in any of the match lists (and lack their metadata); these are left out.

The data are from the repository at commit 4b73468 (7 September 2026).

The networks have the following properties:

- (Node) `player_id`: the StatsBomb id of the player.
- (Node) `name`.
- (Node) `nickname` (empty if none).
- (Node) `country`: the country of the player.
- (Node) `team`: the team(s) for which the player made or received passes in the competition (separated by semicolons).
- (Node) `position`: the position in which the player most often started their appearances (according to the lineups).
- (Edge) `weight`: the number of completed passes.
- (Edge) `match_id`: the StatsBomb id of the match.
- (Edge) `time`: the date of the match (Unix time, at midnight UTC).
- (Edge) `season`: the season of the competition (e.g. "2015/2016" or "2022").
- (Edge) `match`: the label of the match (home and away teams, and final score).
- (Edge) `team`: the team of the two players.
